<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad ibn Suri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1011">1011</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad ibn Suri was a chieftain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghor</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and an ancestor of the Ghurid dynasty. In 1011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> invaded Ghor and captured him; he died in captivity, and his son was left to rule Ghor as a Ghaznavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. Later Ghurid tradition, recorded by Juzjani, gives him a prominent place in the family's history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-suri.jpg" alt="Muhammad ibn Suri" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rashid-al-Din Hamadani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MuhammadibnSuriPaintingHistoryofIran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
